# Jett.io Development Rules

## Project Overview
Building a static Next.js blog (jett.io) for engineering leadership content. Focus: AI, DevOps, Security, Resilience. Voice: Opinionated, practical, action-oriented.

## Brand Identity
- **Positioning**: Battle-tested engineering practices from someone shipping AI at scale in enterprise
- **Voice**: Cut through hype, provide actionable insights, challenge conventional wisdom
- **Tagline**: "Engineering Leadership in the Age of AI: Signal Over Noise"

## Design System

### Colors
- Primary Background: `#000000` (true black)
- Secondary Background: `#0D0D0D` (near black)
- Accent: `#00FF41` (terminal green)
- Text Primary: `#FFFFFF`
- Text Body: `#E5E7EB`
- Text Secondary: `#9CA3AF`
- Code blocks: Green text on black background with glow effects

### Typography
- Font: Inter or system font stack
- Code: JetBrains Mono
- Headings: Bold, high contrast
- Body: 18px, line-height 1.8
- Terminal green glow on accents: `text-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0, 255, 65, 0.3)`

### Visual Style
- High contrast, terminal/hacker aesthetic
- Minimal, clean layouts
- Glowing green accents (subtle box-shadow and text-shadow)
- Spacious, scannable for busy leaders
- Fast loading, performance-first

## Code Conventions

### Next.js Best Practices
- Use App Router (not Pages Router)
- Static export: `output: 'export'` in next.config.js
- No client-side runtime dependencies where possible
- Server Components by default, 'use client' only when necessary
- Optimize images with `unoptimized: true` for static export

### Component Structure
- Use TypeScript for all components
- Functional components with modern React patterns
- Co-locate styles with components (CSS modules or Tailwind)
- Keep components small and focused

### File Organization
```
/app - Next.js app router pages
/components - Reusable UI components
/content/posts - MDX blog posts
/lib - Utilities, helpers
/public - Static assets
/styles - Global styles
```

### Blog Post Format
- MDX files in `/content/posts/`
- Frontmatter required: title, date, excerpt, category, readTime, author
- Categories: AI, DevOps, Security, Resilience
- Every post should end with an "Action Box" component

### Key Components to Maintain
- **ActionBox**: Green-bordered callout for "What You Should Do Monday Morning"
- **CodeBlock**: Terminal green syntax highlighting with glow
- **PostCard**: For blog listings
- **Navigation**: Clean, minimal with logo
- **Logo**: "J" with ascending signal bars

## Content Guidelines

### Writing Style
- Start with bold, provocative statements
- Challenge conventional wisdom
- Focus on implementation over theory
- Include real examples (anonymized if needed)
- Always end with actionable steps
- Short paragraphs, scannable
- Use "signal" vs "noise" framing

### Post Structure
1. Hook with controversial/bold take
2. "The Noise" - what everyone says
3. "The Signal" - what actually works
4. Action Box - what to do Monday morning

## Technical Requirements

### Performance
- Optimize for Core Web Vitals
- Lazy load images
- Minimize JavaScript bundle
- Fast build times

### SEO
- Proper meta tags on all pages
- OpenGraph images
- Sitemap generation
- RSS feed
- Semantic HTML

### Accessibility
- High contrast ratios (already achieved with black/white/green)
- Semantic HTML elements
- Alt text for images
- Keyboard navigation support

## Deployment
- GitHub Pages via GitHub Actions
- Static HTML output
- Custom domain: jett.io
- Auto-deploy on push to main

## Development Workflow
- Test locally before pushing: `npm run dev`
- Build and check static export: `npm run build`
- Keep dependencies minimal
- Document significant changes in commits

## Things to Avoid
- Don't use localStorage/sessionStorage (GitHub Pages limitation)
- No runtime API calls (static site)
- Don't over-engineer - keep it simple
- Avoid unnecessary animations (subtle only)
- No generic stock photos - use code examples, diagrams, or skip images

## Brand Voice Examples

❌ Bad: "In this blog post, I'll explore some thoughts about AI agents..."
✅ Good: "Everyone's wrong about AI agents. Here's what actually works in production."

❌ Bad: "There are many approaches to implementing MLOps..."
✅ Good: "Most MLOps frameworks are overkill. Here's the minimal stack that ships."

## When Making Changes
- Maintain terminal green aesthetic throughout
- Keep code blocks authentic (real, working examples)
- Ensure every feature drives toward the goal: empowering leaders to take action
- Ask: "Would this make a busy engineering manager stop and think?"
